IP TO COUNTRY DATA NOTICE (not bundled — see below)

`country` resolution (§10's `country_db` setting) reads an offline
IP-to-country database in the MMDB binary format, via the `maxminddb`
crate listed below. No such database is bundled, vendored into this
source tree, or shipped in any release artifact (zip, RPM, or
otherwise) — unlike SQLite/SQLCipher above, this is not third-party
code linked into a foSSH binary. It is data the operator downloads
separately and points `country_db` at (`fossh.toml`, or
`FOSSH_COUNTRY_DB`); an install with nothing configured there does no
GeoIP resolution at all and is unaffected by anything in this section.

The database this project's own default configuration guidance points
operators at is db-ip.com's free "IP to Country Lite" database,
licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
License (CC BY 4.0). Per db-ip.com's own license terms: "You are free
to use this IP to Country Lite database in your application, provided
you give attribution to DB-IP.com for the data." Accordingly:

IP Geolocation by DB-IP — https://db-ip.com

This attribution covers use of DB-IP's data by any foSSH deployment
that has configured `country_db` to point at a db-ip.com-sourced
database; it is not a claim that DB-IP's data or service is bundled
with or endorses foSSH itself. An operator who instead points
`country_db` at a different MMDB-format database (e.g. a
commercially-licensed MaxMind GeoIP2 database) is responsible for that
database's own attribution/license terms, which are between the
operator and that database's publisher, not covered here.

